  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 226 PM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nAn upper level shortwave translates over the Desert Southwest \nthrough the weekend, gradually lifting from SoCal/AZ toward the \ncentral Plains. As this system nears, disturbances within \nsouthwesterly flow aloft pair with increased moisture to bring \nincreasing rain chances and a potential for flash flooding over SE\nNM. Isolated to scattered (20-60% chance) showers/storms are \nforecast over the western half of our region Saturday. Rain \nchances increase and spread nearly areawide Saturday evening into\nSunday, when coverage becomes scattered to numerous. The best \nrain chances will tend to favor SE NM and our westernmost \ncounties over the weekend. Rounds of occasionally heavy rainfall \nover portions of SE NM may result in localized flash flooding, \nespecially on Saturday. We will refrain from issuing a Flash Flood\nWatch for SE NM on Saturday with this forecast package, but may \nreconsider within the next few issuances. Stay tuned for any \nupdates! Given the proximity of an upper ridge to our east, \nrainfall over eastern portions of our area look to be relatively \nlighter. The main shortwave lifts into the central and northern \nPlains Monday into Tuesday, sending a cold front southward in its \nwake. Upper troughing remains in place early next week, before \nsubtle ridging builds back in around mid week. Thus, additional \ndisturbances keep at least low rain chances in the forecast \nthrough Tuesday. \n\nAside from active weather, the Long Term period features a gradual \ncooling trend due to lowering heights, cloudier skies, and wetter \nconditions. Highs Saturday are forecast to reach the 70s to mid 80s \n(primarily in/near the river valleys). Highs in the 70s become more \nprevalent Sunday onward, with upper 60s creeping into our northern \ncounties.",
